{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"coleridge","name":"Lake Coleridge","operator":"Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"Lake Coleridge feeds the power station on the Rakaia through intake tunnels; its natural outlet, Lake Stream (to the Harper), is dammed and spills only in lake floods. The Harper Diversion takes the Harper River (up to 40 m3/s, no residual flow required) by canal to the lake; the Oakden Bank below it takes what is left of the Harper (up to 50 m3/s) into the Oakden Canal, which also carries the Wilberforce Canal (up to 30 m3/s; at least 3 m3/s stays in the Wilberforce below its diversion bank). Positions and rates: Environment Canterbury consents (Open Data Portal, read 2026-11-27, rf-6u2). EA Report 4 (3.6, p. 22, Figure 3-8) confirms the layout: the Harper Diversion intake above the Oakden Canal intake on the Harper, the Wilberforce Canal intake (1977) joining the Oakden Canal, the Acheron Diversion (1932), and the natural outlet \"regulated by a control gate\", with spill seldom (some as reverse flow through the Oakden Canal); it gives no diversion rates. EA Report 2 (2.2.6, p. 22): \"Diversions from the Harper and Wilberforce rivers cease during floods when the diversion bunds are washed away\" (the shares describe normal flows). Not modelled: the Acheron diversion (pipeline and race to the lake).","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/2_Flows_20241231/HMD%20Report%202%20-%20Flow%20Series%20Description%20and%20Methodology.pdf","https://www.ecan.govt.nz/data/consent-search/consentdetails/CRC173079","https://www.ecan.govt.nz/data/consent-search/consentdetails/CRC173064","https://www.ecan.govt.nz/data/consent-search/consentdetails/CRC172958","https://www.ecan.govt.nz/data/consent-search/consentdetails/CRC173092","https://data.ecan.govt.nz/Catalogue/Method?MethodId=2","https://braidedrivers.org/rivers/rakaia/"]},{"id":"opuha","name":"Opuha Dam","operator":"Opuha Water","note":"Storage dam for irrigation and power (Lake Opuha); the station is at the dam toe.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand"]},{"id":"waitaki","name":"Waitaki hydro scheme","operator":"Meridian Energy; Tekapo A and B: Genesis Energy","note":"Lake Tekapo goes by canal to Tekapo B and Lake Pukaki; Pukaki and Ohau go by canals to Ohau A (Lake Ruataniwha) and Ohau B and C (Lake Benmore). The Tekapo, Pukaki and Ohau rivers below their control structures carry only residual flows down to Lake Benmore. Benmore, Aviemore and Waitaki are storage dams; the Waitaki below is regulated to the sea.","src":["https://www.meridianenergy.co.nz/power-stations/hydro/about-the-waitaki-hydro-scheme","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waitaki_River"]}],"r":[[84,3,2,100],[89,3,2,100],[90,3,2,100],[91,3,2,100],[92,3,2,100],[128,3,2,100],[129,3,2,100],[130,3,2,100],[170,3,2,100],[171,3,2,100],[215,3,2,100],[216,3,2,100],[239,3,2,100],[240,3,2,100],[241,3,2,100],[242,3,2,100],[318,3,2,100],[319,3,2,100],[377,3,2,100],[400,3,2,100],[401,3,2,100],[402,3,2,100],[403,3,2,100],[404,3,2,100],[405,3,2,100],[406,3,2,100],[407,3,2,100],[427,3,2,100],[428,3,2,100],[429,3,2,100],[441,3,2,100],[442,3,2,100],[443,3,2,100],[444,3,2,100],[542,3,2,100],[552,3,2,100],[569,3,2,100],[577,3,2,100],[600,3,2,100],[606,3,2,100],[607,3